<interrogationes unitas="credo">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write a new test suite with credo?
    <responsum symbolum="credo_suitam_currere CredoSectio CredoContextus" via="probationes/probatio_admonitiones.c">Use
      credo v2: a static CredoSectio table of rows {titulus, probare,
      parare, purgare, signa, exspectata_fracta} ending in a row whose
      titulus is NIHIL, and principale returns
      credo_suitam_currere("suita", SECTIONES). Each section gets its
      own piscina in c-&gt;piscina (destroyed after purgare), runs
      parare, probare, then purgare (always), and the function returns
      0 only if every section that ran passed. CREDO_SECTIO=titulus
      runs one section; an unknown title fails the suite before
      anything runs.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I end an old-style suite so the exit code reflects failures?
    <responsum symbolum="credo_aperire credo_omnia_praeterierunt credo_imprimere_compendium credo_claudere" via="probationes/probatio_piscina.c lib/credo.worklog.md">Ask
      credo_omnia_praeterierunt() BEFORE credo_claudere, print
      credo_imprimere_compendium, and return 0 or 1 from that answer.
      credo_claudere resets all counts, so asking after it sees zero
      assertions, which now reads as FALSUM ("NIHIL PROBATUM"). An
      assertion before credo_aperire prints FATALE and exits the
      process with 1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does a test with zero assertions fail?
    <responsum symbolum="credo_omnia_praeterierunt credo_suitam_currere" via="lib/credo.worklog.md">Because
      "nothing failed" is not "something passed": a moved fixture
      directory or a corpus loop that finds nothing used to exit green.
      credo_omnia_praeterierunt returns FALSUM when the total is 0, a
      v2 section with no assertions is VACUA (not passing), and a suite
      in which no section ran (all OMISSA, say) also returns
      I.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I compare two chorda values with CREDO_CHORDAE_AEQUALES?
    <responsum symbolum="_credo_notare_chorda _credo_notare_chorda_literis chorda_aequalis">No:
      despite the name, CREDO_CHORDAE_AEQUALES takes two C strings
      (constans character*). For chordae use CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS(a,
      b), which wraps chorda_aequalis in CREDO_VERUM, so a failure
      prints only the expression text, never the two values;
      for a chorda against a literal use
      CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(s, "text"), which prints the
      received chorda (first 255 bytes) next to the
      expectation.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I read the Speratus and Receptus lines of a failure?
    <responsum symbolum="_credo_notare _credo_notare_integrum">The
      comparison macros take (received, expected): CREDO_AEQUALIS_S32(a,
      b) prints a as Receptus and b as Speratus, so put the computed
      value first and the expected constant second. Every failure line
      is flushed immediately, so it survives a crash a few lines later
      even through a pipe. Passing assertions print a dot with no
      newline, so a failure's F, or any line the test prints itself,
      follows the dots on the same line: never grep output with a
      leading ^.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I trust the numbers CREDO_AEQUALIS_I32 prints for large unsigned values?
    <responsum symbolum="_credo_notare_integrum_i32 _credo_notare_integrum_inaequalitas_i32 _credo_notare_integrum_maior_i32">The
      verdict yes, the message no. i32 is unsigned, but the equality
      and inequality helpers format it with %d, so a value at or above
      2^31 prints as a negative number; the MAIOR/MINOR _I32 helpers
      use %u and print correctly. For 64 bits only CREDO_AEQUALIS_S64
      exists (signed).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I stop a section early when a precondition fails instead of crashing on NIHIL?
    <responsum symbolum="_credo_necesse credo_suitam_currere">CREDO_NECESSE(x)
      or CREDO_NECESSE_NON_NIHIL(p): it records the assertion and, if
      it failed, longjmps out of the section (marked ABORTA; purgare
      still runs). Outside a section it behaves like CREDO_VERUM. For a
      bail-out site with no expression (fixture missing, file not
      opened), CREDO_CULPA("reason") records an unconditional failure
      so the summary and exit code agree; it does not jump, so you
      still return yourself.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I test that code crashes, or does not crash or hang?
    <responsum symbolum="credo_processus_incipere credo_processus_iudicare credo_processus_filium_finire">CREDO_NON_RUIT(expr),
      CREDO_NON_PENDET(expr, mora_ms) and CREDO_RUIT_CUM(expr, SIGSEGV)
      (or _INTRA with a deadline) run expr in a forked child; the
      parent waits, kills it with SIGKILL past the deadline (default
      CREDO_MORA_ORDINARIA_MS, 5000), and judges how it ended. They are
      statement macros on purpose: as a function argument, expr would
      be evaluated, and crash, in the parent. Assertions made inside
      the child are NOT counted by the parent.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can CREDO_NON_RUIT catch a call that exits with an error code?
    <responsum symbolum="credo_processus_iudicare _credo_necesse">No.
      With no expected signal the check passes whenever the child
      exited normally, whatever its exit status, so exit(I) inside the
      expression (for example a fatal piscina_allocare) or a failed
      CREDO_NECESSE in the child (which calls _exit(I)) still passes.
      Also the deadline counts 1 ms sleep iterations, not wall time,
      so real waits run long (the worklog measured about 25%).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I give a test a scratch directory that is cleaned up?
    <responsum symbolum="credo_area CredoContextus">credo_area(c)
      inside a v2 section returns TMPDIR/suite.n.pid (n = the row's
      position in the table, from 1; in parare_suitae/purgare_suitae
      it is TMPDIR/suite.suita.pid), created on the first call; the
      whole tree is deleted after purgare, even after NECESSE or when a
      CREDO_NON_RUIT child created it. It returns NIHIL outside a
      running context or if the directory cannot be made.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I share an expensive fixture across sections or skip slow ones?
    <responsum symbolum="credo_suitam_currere_cum CredoSectio CredoContextus">credo_suitam_currere_cum(suita,
      sectiones, parare_suitae, purgare_suitae) runs parare_suitae once
      (its datum reaches every section as c-&gt;suita, read-only) and
      purgare_suitae always; if the fixture fails no section runs.
      Tag rows with signa from the closed set "lentus rete fenestra
      repositorium" and CREDO_SIGNA_OMITTE="rete" reports them OMISSA;
      an unknown word fails the suite. CREDO_ORDO=mixtus shuffles the
      sections (seed printed) to find hidden shared state.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I mark a section as a known failure without turning the suite red?
    <responsum symbolum="CredoSectio credo_suitam_currere">Write
      the reason in the row's exspectata_fracta field (an empty string
      is refused before anything runs). A failing or aborted section
      is then NOTA_FRACTA and counts as passing; if it starts passing it
      becomes INOPINATA and fails the suite, telling you to delete the
      line. CREDO_GRADUS("step") names the step inside a section so
      the first failure says which one.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
